<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-sequential-convergence-in-the-infinity-metric" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Sequential convergence in the infinity metric
  </title>
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m> m \in \N </m> be a natural number, and consider a sequence <m> \of{ \vect{ x }_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> of points <m> \vect{ x }_{ n } = \of{ x_{ 1 , n } , \dotsc , x_{ m , n } } \in \R^{ m } </m>. Prove that for any vector <m> \vect{ x } = \of{ x_{ 1 } , \dotsc , x_{ m } } \in \R </m>, the following are equivalent:
      
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            The sequence <m> \of{ \vect{ x }_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> \vect{ x } </m> in the <m> \infty </m>-metric <m> d_{ \infty } </m> on <m> \R^{ m } </m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            For all indices <m> 1 \leq j \leq m </m>, the sequence <m> \of{ x_{ j , n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> x_{ j } </m> in the Euclidean metric <m> d </m> on <m> \R </m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  
  <hint>
    <p>
      You may want to revisit the proof of <xref ref="theorem-sequential-convergence-in-the-p-metric"/> for inspiration.
    </p>
  </hint>
  
  <solution>
    <p>
      First suppose that the sequence <m> \of{ \vect{ x }_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> \vect{ x } </m> in the <m> \infty </m>-metric <m> d_{ \infty } </m> on <m> \R^{ m } </m>. Fix an index <m> 1 \leq j \leq m </m>, and let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. Then there is a natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m> so that
      
      <md>
        d_{ \infty } \of{ \vect{ x }_{ n } , \vect{ x } } \lt \epsilon
      </md>
      
      for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>. We observe that
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          d \of{ x_{ j , n } , x_{ j } } \amp = \abs{ x_{ j , n } - x_{ j } } \leq \max_{ 1 \leq k \leq m }{ \abs{ x_{ k , n } - x_{ k } } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = \norm{ \vect{ x }_{ n } - \vect{ x } }_{ \infty } = d_{ \infty } \of{ \vect{ x }_{ n } , \vect{ x } } \lt \epsilon
        </mrow>
      </md>
      
      for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>, and so <m> \of{ x_{ j , n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> x_{ j } </m>.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      Conversely, now suppose that for all indices <m> 1 \leq j \leq m </m>, the sequence <m> \of{ x_{ j , n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> x_{ j } </m> in the Euclidean metric <m> d </m> on <m> \R </m>. Let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. Then for each index <m> 1 \leq j \leq m </m>, there is a natural number <m> n_{ j , \epsilon } \in \N </m> so that
      
      <md>
        d \of{ x_{ j , n } , x_{ j } } \lt \epsilon
      </md>
      
      for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ j , \epsilon } </m>. Let <m> n_{ \epsilon } = \max_{ 1 \leq j \leq m }{ n_{ j , \epsilon } } \in \N </m>, and note that
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          d_{ \infty } \of{ \vect{ x }_{ n } , \vect{ x } } \amp = \norm{ \vect{ x }_{ n } - \vect{ x } }_{ \infty } = \max_{ 1 \leq j \leq m }{ \abs{ x_{ j , n } - x_{ j } } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = \max_{ 1 \leq j \leq m }{ d \of{ x_{ j , n } , x_{ j } } } \lt \max_{ 1 \leq j \leq m }{ \epsilon } = \epsilon
        </mrow>
      </md>
      
      for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>, and so the sequence <m> \of{ \vect{ x }_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> \vect{ x } </m> in the <m> \infty </m>-metric <m> d_{ \infty } </m> on <m> \R^{ m } </m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>
